# `SEQUENCE-CONTAINMENT-SCOPE`: the ONE-LEVEL NESTING CAP, which the prototype did not
# have, and the CONTIGUITY rule, which it did.
#
# The cap is the v0.1 `group` precedent — one level is the whole of the
# language's containment — and it is a SCOPE decision, not a principle: the
# message says so, and says a second level lands on measured need.
#
# Contiguity is a MODEL rule and not a drawing rule: an operand denotes the
# ORDERED RUN of the occurrences it contains, so an occurrence that is not in
# it cannot happen between two that are. One offending line is reported ONCE,
# against the DEEPEST container it splits, because that is the container whose
# `in=` repairs the document — reporting the outer one too would give advice
# that fixes the fragment and leaves the operand split.
figdown 0.4 sequence
lifeline c "C"
lifeline s "S"
fragment f1 "level 0" type=alt
operand o1 "a" in=f1
message c -> s "one" in=o1
message c -> s "this line splits the run"
message c -> s "two" in=o1
fragment f2 "level 1 — legal" type=opt in=o1
operand o2 "b" in=f2
message c -> s "three" in=o2
fragment f3 "level 2 — one too deep" type=loop in=o2
operand o3 "c" in=f3
message c -> s "four" in=o3
